"Atoms view: {#} argon atoms that attract each other at a distance and repel when they touch (the Lennard-Jones potential, with argon's bond strength ε/k = {#} K and size σ = {#} nm, cut off at {#} nm). Newton's second law moves every atom in steps of {#} fs. The sample repeats sideways, so an atom leaving one side comes back in at the other, as if it were part of a wide sheet. A free piston presses down with the pressure you set, and the heater adds energy to the atoms touching the floor. The piston is idealized: it pushes with exactly the set pressure (its gauge shows it), with no weights, from {#} to {#} atm. At low pressure the vapor needs far more room, so the picture squeezes heights above {#} nm (a dashed line marks where) to keep the piston in view; the Column height readout is the true height. The column stops at {#} nm, so at {#} atm the vapor fills it and then the volume stays fixed. Temperature is the average kinetic energy of the atoms (KE = {#}/{#} kT per atom); the bond energy U is their potential energy, negative because bound atoms have less energy than separated ones. The heat added equals the change in KE + U + PV: the first law at constant pressure. The run lasts nanoseconds, so the heater is far stronger than a stove. Two things move the temperatures away from real argon's {#} K (melting) and {#} K (boiling at {#} atm). The setups use {#} atm, not {#} atm, so that the vapor stays near the sample (at {#} atm it would need more than ten times the room), and a liquid boils hotter at a higher pressure: that is why boiling here comes well above {#} K. And the sample is only a few nanometers thick, with many atoms at a surface, and each atom feels only the atoms within {#} nm, so it is held together less firmly than a flask of argon: it melts below {#} K and its latent heats are smaller. The shape of the heating curve, and where the heat goes, are the same. A small sample can also overheat for a moment before the first vapor forms, then settle back to the boiling plateau; real liquids do this too (superheating). Temperatures and energies on screen are averages over the last {#} ns. The flat view shows a slice {#} nm thick through the middle of the sample, with the atoms behind it faint; the 3D view shows them all.",
